In the daily standup, it was noted that the timezone settings across the Nautilus Application Servers in the Stratos Datacenter are inconsistent with the local datacenter's timezone, currently set to Asia/Manila.

Synchronize the timezone settings to match the local datacenter's timezone (Asia/Manila).

SOLUTION
To synchronize the timezone on the Nautilus Application Servers to match the datacenter's local timezone (Asia/Manila), you will use the timedatectl command. This change takes effect immediately without requiring a reboot.

🛠️ Configuration Steps
Execute the following commands on each App server:

1. Set the timezone to Asia/Manila

bash
sudo timedatectl set-timezone Asia/Manila
2. Verify the change

bash
timedatectl
Expected output (key lines):

text
               Local time: <current time> PST
           Universal time: <current time> UTC
                 RTC time: <current time>
                Time zone: Asia/Manila (PST, +0800)
System clock synchronized: yes
              NTP service: active
          RTC in local TZ: no
You can also verify with a quick date check:

bash
date
The output should show the PST (Philippine Standard Time, UTC+8) suffix.

🔄 Alternative Method (If timedatectl Is Unavailable)
If for some reason timedatectl is not available, you can use the traditional method:

bash
sudo ln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/Asia/Manila /etc/localtime
sudo hwclock --systohc
The first command replaces the symlink so the system reads timezone data from the correct file.

The second command writes the current system time to the hardware clock so the change persists across reboots.

Verify with:

bash
date
ls -l /etc/localtime
The symlink should point to /usr/share/zoneinfo/Asia/Manila.

📋 Verification Checklist
Check	Command	Expected Result
Timezone is set	timedatectl	Time zone: Asia/Manila (PST, +0800)
Date reflects PST	date	Time with PST suffix
Symlink correct (alt method)	ls -l /etc/localtime	Points to .../zoneinfo/Asia/Manila
⚠️ Important Notes
No Reboot Required: timedatectl set-timezone applies the change immediately to the running system.

NTP Sync: If System clock synchronized: no, ensure systemd-timesyncd or chronyd is running so the clock stays accurate:

bash
sudo systemctl enable --now systemd-timesyncd
# or
sudo systemctl enable --now chronyd
Repeat on All Servers: Apply these commands on every App server in the Stratos Datacenter to ensure consistency across the fleet (App Server 1, 2, 3, etc.).

Application Impact: Some applications may cache timezone data. If an app reads the timezone only at startup, it may need a service restart to pick up the new setting:

bash
sudo systemctl restart <service-name>
